  "sourceLocator": {
    "chapter": "Playing the Game",
    "section": "1.83",
    "heading": "Controlling a Mount",
    "lineStart": 699,
    "lineEnd": 705
  },
  "section": "1.83",
  "rulesText": "You can control a mount only if it has been trained to accept a rider. Domesticated horses, mules, and similar creatures have such training.\n\nThe Initiative of a controlled mount changes to match yours when you mount it. It moves on your turn as you direct it, and it has only three action options during that turn: Dash, Disengage, and Dodge. A controlled mount can move and act even on the turn that you mount it.\n\nIn contrast, an independent mount - one that lets you ride but ignores your control - retains its place in the Initiative order and moves and acts as it likes."
}
